1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,706
<font color="#FFFFFF">Spiking Neural Networks promise low-power AI,</font>

2
00:00:03,786 --> 00:00:08,161
<font color="#FFFFFF">but training them over many timesteps is expensive
in memory,</font>

3
00:00:08,241 --> 00:00:11,302
<font color="#FFFFFF">and running them repeats work at inference.</font>

4
00:00:11,382 --> 00:00:14,296
<font color="#FFFFFF">This ICML 2024 paper introduces T-RevSNN,</font>

5
00:00:14,376 --> 00:00:20,650
<font color="#FFFFFF">a Temporal Reversible architecture that turns off
the temporal dynamics of most spiking</font>

6
00:00:20,730 --> 00:00:25,616
<font color="#FFFFFF">neurons and makes the few remaining temporal
connections reversible.</font>

7
00:00:25,696 --> 00:00:29,925
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result is O(L) training memory and O(1)
inference cost,</font>

8
00:00:30,005 --> 00:00:37,886
<font color="#FFFFFF">with state-of-the-art accuracy among CNN-based SNNs
on ImageNet and up to 8.6 times better memory efficiency,</font>

9
00:00:37,966 --> 00:00:42,560
<font color="#FFFFFF">2.0 times faster training, and 1.6 times lower
inference energy.</font>

10
00:00:42,940 --> 00:00:46,200
<font color="#FFFFFF">Spiking Neural Networks are appealing because they
promise brain-inspired,</font>

11
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:49,540
<font color="#FFFFFF">low-power computation.</font>

12
00:00:49,620 --> 00:00:52,880
<font color="#FFFFFF">But to work well they are simulated over many timesteps,</font>

13
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:56,220
<font color="#FFFFFF">and that comes at a steep cost.</font>

14
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:59,560
<font color="#FFFFFF">During training,</font>

15
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:02,900
<font color="#FFFFFF">memory grows with both the number of layers and the
number of timesteps,</font>

16
00:01:02,980 --> 00:01:06,240
<font color="#FFFFFF">on the order of L times T.</font>

17
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:09,580
<font color="#FFFFFF">At inference, repeating the input over T steps
makes the energy scale with T as well.</font>

18
00:01:09,660 --> 00:01:12,920
<font color="#FFFFFF">The frustrating part is that current training
methods can relieve one of these pressures</font>

19
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,260
<font color="#FFFFFF">but not the other at the same time,</font>

20
00:01:16,340 --> 00:01:19,600
<font color="#FFFFFF">leaving SNNs stuck with a training memory and
inference energy dilemma.</font>

21
00:01:19,980 --> 00:01:23,951
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key insight behind this work is surprisingly simple.</font>

22
00:01:24,031 --> 00:01:28,002
<font color="#FFFFFF">When the authors examined the gradients that flow
backward through time in a spiking network,</font>

23
00:01:28,082 --> 00:01:32,053
<font color="#FFFFFF">they found that for most neurons those temporal
gradients barely matter.</font>

24
00:01:32,133 --> 00:01:36,104
<font color="#FFFFFF">Only the neurons at a few key positions carry
temporal information that is actually important.</font>

25
00:01:36,184 --> 00:01:40,155
<font color="#FFFFFF">If that is true,</font>

26
00:01:40,235 --> 00:01:44,206
<font color="#FFFFFF">then a natural question follows: why pay the full
temporal cost for every neuron?</font>

27
00:01:44,286 --> 00:01:48,257
<font color="#FFFFFF">Prior methods either decouple training from the
timestep to save memory,</font>

28
00:01:48,337 --> 00:01:52,308
<font color="#FFFFFF">or shrink the number of inference steps to save energy,</font>

29
00:01:52,388 --> 00:01:56,359
<font color="#FFFFFF">but each one solves only half of the problem.</font>

30
00:01:56,439 --> 00:02:00,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper asks whether both halves can be solved
together.</font>

31
00:02:00,790 --> 00:02:05,013
<font color="#FFFFFF">Their answer is T-RevSNN, a Temporal Reversible
architecture for spiking networks.</font>

32
00:02:05,093 --> 00:02:09,315
<font color="#FFFFFF">The core idea is to turn off the temporal dynamics
of most spiking neurons and keep them</font>

33
00:02:09,395 --> 00:02:13,618
<font color="#FFFFFF">on only at a few key positions,</font>

34
00:02:13,698 --> 00:02:17,921
<font color="#FFFFFF">where the temporal connections are made reversible.</font>

35
00:02:18,001 --> 00:02:22,224
<font color="#FFFFFF">Reversibility means the network can recompute
activations during the backward pass instead of storing them,</font>

36
00:02:22,304 --> 00:02:26,526
<font color="#FFFFFF">which brings training memory down to order L.</font>

37
00:02:26,606 --> 00:02:30,829
<font color="#FFFFFF">On top of that,</font>

38
00:02:30,909 --> 00:02:35,132
<font color="#FFFFFF">they encode the input image only once and split
both the features and the network into independent sub-networks,</font>

39
00:02:35,212 --> 00:02:39,435
<font color="#FFFFFF">so inference cost becomes constant, order one.</font>

40
00:02:39,515 --> 00:02:43,737
<font color="#FFFFFF">To make this sparse temporal design actually train well,</font>

41
00:02:43,817 --> 00:02:48,040
<font color="#FFFFFF">they also redesign the basic SNN block in a
ConvNeXt style and add a scaled residual connection.</font>

42
00:02:48,420 --> 00:02:52,039
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here is how it works in practice.</font>

43
00:02:52,119 --> 00:02:55,739
<font color="#FFFFFF">Instead of feeding the same image to the network at
every timestep,</font>

44
00:02:55,819 --> 00:02:59,438
<font color="#FFFFFF">T-RevSNN encodes the image just once, divides the
encoded features into T groups,</font>

45
00:02:59,518 --> 00:03:03,137
<font color="#FFFFFF">and gives one group to each timestep.</font>

46
00:03:03,217 --> 00:03:06,836
<font color="#FFFFFF">The whole network is likewise split into T
sub-networks that share parameters and exchange</font>

47
00:03:06,916 --> 00:03:10,536
<font color="#FFFFFF">temporal information only at the key,</font>

48
00:03:10,616 --> 00:03:14,235
<font color="#FFFFFF">turned-on neurons.</font>

49
00:03:14,315 --> 00:03:17,934
<font color="#FFFFFF">Those key connections follow a multi-level
temporal-reversible rule: the membrane potential</font>

50
00:03:18,014 --> 00:03:21,634
<font color="#FFFFFF">at one timestep can be exactly reconstructed from
the next timestep's state and the incoming</font>

51
00:03:21,714 --> 00:03:25,333
<font color="#FFFFFF">spikes.</font>

52
00:03:25,413 --> 00:03:29,032
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because the forward pass is reversible,</font>

53
00:03:29,112 --> 00:03:32,731
<font color="#FFFFFF">the backward pass only needs the membrane
potentials of the final timestep,</font>

54
00:03:32,811 --> 00:03:36,431
<font color="#FFFFFF">so intermediate activations do not have to be stored.</font>

55
00:03:36,511 --> 00:03:40,130
<font color="#FFFFFF">That is what collapses the training memory from
order L times T down to order L.</font>

56
00:03:40,510 --> 00:03:45,358
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method is evaluated on both static and
neuromorphic vision.</font>

57
00:03:45,438 --> 00:03:50,756
<font color="#FFFFFF">For static images they use ImageNet-1K at a
resolution of 224 by 224.</font>

58
00:03:50,836 --> 00:03:56,466
<font color="#FFFFFF">For event-based data they use CIFAR10-DVS and the
DVS128 Gesture dataset.</font>

59
00:03:56,546 --> 00:04:01,786
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially, they report not just accuracy but the
real training cost,</font>

60
00:04:01,866 --> 00:04:10,391
<font color="#FFFFFF">measuring peak GPU memory per image and per-epoch
training time on six NVIDIA A100 GPUs under mixed precision.</font>

61
00:04:10,471 --> 00:04:16,101
<font color="#FFFFFF">They compare against a broad set of baselines,
including spiking ResNets,</font>

62
00:04:16,181 --> 00:04:22,437
<font color="#FFFFFF">spiking Transformers, and other
training-optimization methods such as OTTT, SLTT,</font>

63
00:04:22,517 --> 00:04:25,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the spatially reversible S-RevSNN.</font>

64
00:04:25,790 --> 00:04:28,314
<font color="#FFFFFF">The results on ImageNet are strong.</font>

65
00:04:28,394 --> 00:04:32,182
<font color="#FFFFFF">With about 30 million parameters and four timesteps,</font>

66
00:04:32,262 --> 00:04:38,580
<font color="#FFFFFF">T-RevSNN reaches 73.2 percent top-1 accuracy while
using only 85.7 megabytes of memory</font>

67
00:04:38,660 --> 00:04:42,300
<font color="#FFFFFF">per image and 2.8 millijoules of inference energy.</font>

68
00:04:42,380 --> 00:04:46,912
<font color="#FFFFFF">That is the best accuracy among convolutional
spiking ResNets,</font>

69
00:04:46,992 --> 00:04:52,268
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it comes with the lowest training memory, the
fastest training time,</font>

70
00:04:52,348 --> 00:04:55,616
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the lowest inference energy in its class.</font>

71
00:04:55,696 --> 00:05:00,600
<font color="#FFFFFF">Compared against a leading spiking Transformer at
similar accuracy,</font>

72
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:04,989
<font color="#FFFFFF">T-RevSNN uses 8.6 times less memory, trains 2 times
faster,</font>

73
00:05:05,069 --> 00:05:08,188
<font color="#FFFFFF">and spends 1.6 times less inference energy.</font>

74
00:05:08,268 --> 00:05:16,520
<font color="#FFFFFF">A lighter 15 million parameter version still
reaches nearly 70 percent accuracy at under 60 megabytes per image.</font>

75
00:05:16,900 --> 00:05:21,019
<font color="#FFFFFF">The ablations show that each design choice earns
its place.</font>

76
00:05:21,099 --> 00:05:27,353
<font color="#FFFFFF">Simply applying temporal reversibility to a
standard MS-ResNet-34 slashes training memory</font>

77
00:05:27,433 --> 00:05:33,403
<font color="#FFFFFF">from 267 down to 88 megabytes per image and cuts
epoch time from 11.2 to 7.4 minutes,</font>

78
00:05:33,483 --> 00:05:37,460
<font color="#FFFFFF">at the cost of only about one and a half accuracy points.</font>

79
00:05:37,540 --> 00:05:44,435
<font color="#FFFFFF">The multi-level temporal fusion between stages is
worth roughly 1.2 points of accuracy on its own.</font>

80
00:05:44,515 --> 00:05:48,776
<font color="#FFFFFF">Varying the number of timesteps trades accuracy
against cost,</font>

81
00:05:48,856 --> 00:05:54,328
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the scaled residual connection helps the model
converge noticeably faster,</font>

82
00:05:54,408 --> 00:05:58,313
<font color="#FFFFFF">reaching 60 percent accuracy in 25 epochs instead of 32.</font>

83
00:05:58,393 --> 00:06:06,000
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors also confirm that temporal and spatial
reversibility are orthogonal and can be stacked together.</font>

84
00:06:06,380 --> 00:06:14,317
<font color="#FFFFFF">To summarize the numbers that matter most: against
a leading spiking Transformer at comparable accuracy,</font>

85
00:06:14,397 --> 00:06:20,329
<font color="#FFFFFF">T-RevSNN delivers 8.6 times better memory
efficiency, 2 times faster training,</font>

86
00:06:20,409 --> 00:06:23,181
<font color="#FFFFFF">and 1.6 times lower inference energy.</font>

87
00:06:23,261 --> 00:06:27,344
<font color="#FFFFFF">It reaches 73.2 percent top-1 accuracy on ImageNet-1K,</font>

88
00:06:27,424 --> 00:06:33,511
<font color="#FFFFFF">using just 85.7 megabytes of memory per image and
2.8 millijoules per inference.</font>

89
00:06:33,591 --> 00:06:40,140
<font color="#FFFFFF">And it achieves all of this with order L training
memory and order one inference cost.</font>

90
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:46,032
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting message of this paper is that you do
not need full temporal dynamics everywhere</font>

91
00:06:46,112 --> 00:06:48,183
<font color="#FFFFFF">to build a capable spiking network.</font>

92
00:06:48,263 --> 00:06:51,809
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because most temporal gradients turn out to be
unimportant,</font>

93
00:06:51,889 --> 00:06:57,463
<font color="#FFFFFF">switching off the temporal dynamics of most neurons
and making the few remaining connections</font>

94
00:06:57,543 --> 00:07:02,010
<font color="#FFFFFF">reversible gives you order L training memory and
order one inference cost,</font>

95
00:07:02,090 --> 00:07:03,977
<font color="#FFFFFF">with almost no loss in accuracy.</font>

96
00:07:04,057 --> 00:07:09,385
<font color="#FFFFFF">By removing the memory and training-time bottleneck
that has held spiking networks back,</font>

97
00:07:09,465 --> 00:07:12,580
<font color="#FFFFFF">T-RevSNN opens a path toward larger, more practical,</font>

98
00:07:12,660 --> 00:07:15,530
<font color="#FFFFFF">and more energy-efficient brain-inspired models.</font>
